## 6. Codebase Architecture Snapshot

This section describes the app as it actually exists, so a new session can orient without re-exploring from scratch. Update it when the architecture changes meaningfully-keep it accurate, not exhaustive.

**Stack**: Next.js 16 (App Router, Turbopack), React 19, TypeScript (strict), Tailwind CSS 4, TanStack React Query 5, Zustand 5, socket.io-client 4. No UI component library-bespoke Tailwind components.

**Pages**: `app/page.tsx` (marketing landing page-server-component shell; the root layout locks body scroll for the chat app, so this page owns its own `overflow-y-auto` container with the nav `sticky` inside it), `app/login/page.tsx` (desktop split layout-rotating `FeatureShowcase` on the left, auth form on the right; the showcase column is hidden below `lg` so mobile is the form alone), `app/chat/page.tsx` (client component; redirects to `/login` if unauthenticated, otherwise renders `ChatSidebar` + `ChatWindow` and wires `useSocket(token)` for the whole session).

**Landing page (`components/marketing/`)**: sections are scripted demos styled to match the production chat components (bubble radii, accent-to/surface-elevated coloring, `Avatar`) without importing the stateful production components themselves, since those need real `conversationId`/data-layer wiring the marketing page has none of. The hero (`landing-hero.tsx`, full viewport height via `min-h-[calc(100svh-4rem)]`) is two-column: headline/CTA copy on the left, `hero-live-conversation.tsx` on the right-a single looping scripted 1:1 thread (message → reply → typing indicator → reply) built with `motion/react` bubble entrances (left for the other party, right for you) and a subtle delivered-tick state change, no laptop/phone device chrome. `network-background.tsx` renders a faint fixed-coordinate SVG node graph behind the hero with a couple of signal dots traveling edges via CSS `offset-path` (`animate-network-signal` in `globals.css`)-deliberately hardcoded, not randomized, so SSR and client markup match. All demo timers are gated by `hooks/use-in-viewport.ts` (nothing runs offscreen) and `hooks/use-reduced-motion-safe.ts` collapses playback to the finished state under `prefers-reduced-motion` (also disables the network's signal-dot animation via a `prefers-reduced-motion` media block). Scripted transcripts are `role="img"` with a description. **Hydration rule learned the hard way**: never branch SSR-rendered markup or Motion `initial` values on the raw `useReducedMotion`-the server can't know the preference; keep initial/variant values constant and put reduction only in transition durations, or use `use-reduced-motion-safe.ts` (false during SSR/hydration, real value after) where reduced motion must change the DOM. CTAs link to `/login`.

**Layered structure**:
- `services/api/*.ts`-thin fetch wrappers per resource (`auth`, `conversations`, `messages`, `users`), all routed through `services/api/client.ts`'s `apiRequest<T>()` (adds base URL, auth header, JSON body, and throws a parsed `ApiError` via `services/api/error.ts` on non-2xx).
- `services/socket/client.ts`-a module-level Socket.IO singleton (`connectSocket`/`disconnectSocket`/`getSocket`), not a hook itself.
- `services/config.ts`-`API_BASE_URL`, `SOCKET_URL`, `AUTH_STORAGE_KEY` (env-driven with hardcoded Render fallbacks).
- `hooks/*.ts`-one hook per concern, each composing React Query or Zustand: `use-auth`, `use-conversations`, `use-messages`, `use-send-message` (optimistic send + retry), `use-socket` (realtime subscription + cache patching), `use-auto-scroll` (pure scroll-position logic, no data dependency), `use-start-conversation`, `use-create-group`, `use-group-management` (rename/add/remove/promote/leave, each patching the conversations cache from the returned group), `use-global-health` (the single `/health` poll feeding the connection banner), `use-document-title` (mirrors the unread total into the tab title), `use-user-search`, `use-debounced-value`.
- `store/*.ts`-Zustand, each store minimal and single-purpose: `auth-store` (persisted token only, manual `skipHydration` to avoid SSR localStorage access), `chat-store` (`activeConversationId` only), `connection-store` (browser online / server reachable / socket connected, with `selectConnectionStatus` and `selectIsOffline` selectors), `unread-store` (persisted per-conversation unread records), `toast-store` (transient in-app popups, never persisted). All other state (messages, conversations, scroll position, sending status) intentionally lives in React Query cache or local component state, not in a store.
- `types/*.ts`-domain types shared across layers (`Message`/`ClientMessage`, `Conversation` as a `DirectConversation | GroupConversation` discriminated union, API request/response shapes).
- `utils/*.ts`-pure functions only: `messages.ts` (`normalizeMessages`, `mergeMessage`, `reconcileIncomingMessage`-sorting/dedup for the message cache), `date.ts` (time/day formatting), `sort.ts`, `query-keys.ts` (central React Query key factory, always token-scoped), `phone.ts`.
- `components/chat/*.tsx`-one component per responsibility per the Task 2 pro-tip: `chat-window`, `message-list`, `message-bubble`, `message-composer`, `chat-sidebar`, `start-chat-panel` (tabbed direct/group creation), `group-info-panel` (members + admin actions), `jump-to-bottom-button`, `avatar`.
- `components/marketing/feature-showcase.tsx`-the login page's left column: four product features rotating every 4.2s (paused on hover/focus, dots to jump), each revealed with `StaggeredText`. Not React Bits Pro-that registry needs a paid `REACTBITS_LICENSE_KEY`, so the blur-swipe reveal is implemented locally with Motion.
- `components/ui/*.tsx`-presentational primitives with no domain knowledge: `skeleton`, `wordmark` (the "Taghyeer **Chat**" mark-single source for the nav, login and showcase), `staggered-text` (per-word/per-char blur-swipe reveal via Motion variants; animates only opacity/filter/transform, exposes the whole string once via an `sr-only` span, and collapses to an instant fade under `prefers-reduced-motion`), `modal` (backdrop, click-outside and Escape close; used by both the new-conversation and group-info modals).
- `components/layout/*.tsx`-app chrome: `icon-rail`, `top-bar`, `account-menu`.

**Realtime**: Socket.IO (not polling, not SSE)-confirmed as the only mechanism the backend exposes. `useSocket` listens for `message:new` (patches an already-opened conversation's message cache, invalidates the conversation list), `conversation:updated` (see below), `connect_error`/`disconnect` (flips `socketConnected` in the connection store), and `connect` (invalidates all open message queries to recover from events missed while disconnected, since Socket.IO doesn't redeliver).

**Global connection state**: three signals feed `store/connection-store.ts`-the browser's `online`/`offline` events, a `GET /health` poll (`hooks/use-global-health.ts`, mounted once in `AppProviders`; 30s while healthy, 5s while down, paused when the tab is hidden, skipped entirely when the browser already reports offline), and the Socket.IO connection state. Browser-offline or health-failing means **offline**: `components/feedback/connection-banner.tsx` shows "Connection lost. Attempting to reconnect..." and `MessageComposer` disables Send (the textarea stays enabled so drafts keep saving). A dropped socket alone is only **degraded**-REST sending still works, so it warns ("Live updates paused") without blocking. The banner is intentionally not dismissible: it mirrors live state and clears itself.

**Send transport-socket-first, REST fallback**: `useSendMessage` emits `message:send` over the socket when it is connected, and falls back to `POST /messages` only when it is not. The connected check happens *before* any emit, which is what makes the fallback safe-REST is used only when nothing was sent over the socket. Once an emit is in flight its errors never fall back: a `{ok:false}` rejection means the server refused it, and an ack timeout is ambiguous (the server may have stored and broadcast it), so a REST retry would risk a duplicate. Both surface as `status: "failed"` with the server's own wording and the existing manual retry.

The ack is only `{ok:true}` / `{ok:false,error}`-**never the created message**-so the socket path has no canonical `_id`/`createdAt` to reconcile against. `emitMessageSend` in `services/socket/client.ts` therefore resolves with nothing, and `useSendMessage` settles the optimistic entry to `status: "sent"`, keeping its `temp-` id until the next full refetch of that conversation swaps in the canonical copy (conversation switch, remount, or the reconnect handler in `use-socket.ts`). Deliberately **not** invalidating messages after each socket send-one request per message would defeat the point of using the socket.

**Group changes (`conversation:updated`)**: fires on create/rename/member/admin changes and reaches every participant-the actor included, and a just-removed member too (verified with four sockets). The handler patches the conversations cache from the payload instead of refetching, mirroring `use-group-management.ts`. Cases: **not in `participants`** → drop the conversation, clear it if it was active, toast "You were removed"; **not in cache** → insert it, toast "You were added", and invalidate once (the payload has no `lastMessage`, so an existing group would show an empty preview); **otherwise** → replace in place, toasting only what affects you (rename, or you gaining admin) since other members coming and going is noise.

The payload **omits `updatedAt`, `createdAt` and `lastMessage`** despite API.md's old example-writing it in raw would blank sidebar previews and feed `NaN` to `sortByRecency`. `normalizeSocketConversation` merges those fields from the cached copy and returns `null` for an unusable payload, in which case the handler falls back to the old invalidate-and-refetch. Do not "simplify" that merge away.

**Unread notifications (client-side only)**: the API has no unread count, no read state on a message, and no event but `message:new`-which the server sends to *every* participant regardless of what they have open. `use-socket.ts` is therefore the single write point: on each incoming message it calls `unread-store`'s `noteIncoming` unless you are actively looking at that conversation (active **and** tab visible → `markRead` instead; active but tab hidden still counts). It reads `activeConversationId` via `useChatStore.getState()`, never a subscription-subscribing would put it in the effect's deps and rebuild every socket listener on each conversation switch.

Surfaces: sidebar count pill (`chat-sidebar.tsx`), tab title `(N) Taghyeer Chat` (`use-document-title.ts`), the top-bar bell + `notifications-panel.tsx` (one row **per conversation**, not per message), and `toast-stack.tsx` (Motion, since toasts need exit animations; suppressed while the tab is hidden). The icon rail's formerly-dead "Notifications" item opens the same panel.

Storage is deliberately bounded: `utils/unread-storage.ts` keeps **one record per conversation**-count, the latest preview truncated to 120 chars, sender id (names are resolved at render), and `seenAt`-never a message log. Records are pruned against the conversations list on load. Because counts are only observable live, a message that landed while the app was closed can't be counted: `hasUnseenSince` compares `lastMessage.createdAt` to `seenAt` and the row shows a **dot** instead of a number.

**Gotcha**: React re-applies the route's `<title>` after hydration, silently overwriting a plain `document.title` assignment-so the unread count vanished from the tab title on reload. `use-document-title.ts` re-asserts it with a `MutationObserver` on `<head>`; don't replace that with a bare assignment.

**Optimistic send**: `useSendMessage` adds a `status: "sending"` message with a `temp-<uuid>` id immediately on submit; on the REST path it reconciles to the real server message on success, on the socket path it settles to `status: "sent"` (see above), and either way an error flips it to `status: "failed"` with a retry affordance. Both paths then invalidate the conversations list-**the sender never receives their own `message:new`** (verified against the live API: not on the sending socket, not in their other tabs, and not for REST sends either), so nothing else would refresh the sidebar preview and ordering after you send. `reconcileIncomingMessage` in `utils/messages.ts` is defensive only for the same reason: no self-echo currently exists to race the reconciliation.

**Groups are fully supported** (an earlier version of this file claimed otherwise-it was wrong, and had assumed the wrong endpoint). Group creation is `POST /conversations/group` with `{ name, participantIds }`, documented in `API.md`; `POST /conversations` is the *direct*-only endpoint and does reject `type: "group"`, which is what caused the mistaken conclusion. The server enforces **at least 3 total members**, and `participantIds` excludes the creator-so the UI requires picking 2+ people, and a 1-person selection falls back to creating a direct conversation (see `MIN_GROUP_PARTICIPANTS` in `services/api/conversations.ts`). Creation, group rendering (sender name/avatar in `MessageList`/`MessageBubble`), and realtime group delivery are all verified end-to-end against the live API.

Group management (`POST /conversations/{id}/participants`, `DELETE .../participants/{userId}`, `POST .../admins`, `PATCH /conversations/{id}`) is wired through `use-group-management` into `GroupInfoPanel`, opened from the chat header's kebab. All four are **admins-only** (`403 FORBIDDEN` otherwise), so the UI gates them on `admins.includes(currentUserId)`; "Leave group" is the remove endpoint called with your own id and is available to everyone. Two undocumented behaviours confirmed by testing: removing a member also strips them from `admins`, and every endpoint returns the full updated group (so mutations patch the cache rather than refetching).

**Socket payload quirk**: `message:new` does **not** match the REST message shape-it sends `id` (not `_id`) and `createdAt` as epoch milliseconds (not an ISO string), contrary to what `API.md` shows. `normalizeSocketMessage` in `utils/messages.ts` converts it at the boundary in `use-socket.ts`. This matters: without it `_id` is `undefined`, so `mergeMessage`'s dedup never matches and a socket-delivered message duplicates against its REST copy on the next refetch (and React logs a missing-key warning). Do not remove that normalization.
